# PART 7 — NOTION API INTEGRATION

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

Build a proper Notion integration architecture.

Use the official Notion API and current official API documentation.

Official documentation:
https://developers.notion.com/

## 7.1 OAuth

Implement Notion OAuth using a secure server-side authorization flow.

Requirements:
- Connect Notion button.
- Authorization redirect.
- OAuth callback route.
- State validation.
- Secure token exchange.
- Token storage on the server.
- Connection status.
- Disconnect option.
- Error handling.

Never put a Notion secret or access token in browser code.

Never expose access tokens through frontend environment variables.

## 7.2 Discover customer databases

After a customer connects Notion:

- Retrieve the pages and databases accessible to the integration.
- Identify the eight expected management databases.
- Retrieve their database and data-source information.
- Inspect their actual property names and types.
- Map each database to the correct website module.
- Store the mapping securely for that customer.

Expected database names:

1. Task Manager.
2. Project Manager.
3. Client Manager.
4. Invoice Manager.
5. Income Tracker.
6. Expense Tracker.
7. Time Tracker.
8. Proposal Tracker.

Do not rely exclusively on database names. Customers may rename their databases.

Provide a database mapping screen so the customer can confirm the correct database for each module.

If a database is missing, explain what is missing and how the customer can grant access.

## 7.3 API version and data sources

Use the current supported Notion API version.

Respect the current API distinction between databases and data sources.

Retrieve a database's data-source IDs before querying its records.

Do not mistakenly use a database ID where a data-source ID is required.

Use the correct endpoints for:
- Retrieving databases.
- Retrieving data sources.
- Querying data sources.
- Retrieving pages.
- Creating pages.
- Updating pages.
- Retrieving block content.
- Updating block content when supported.

Handle pagination.

Handle API rate limits and retry responses appropriately.

Do not assume every Notion property type can be read or written in exactly the same way.

## 7.4 Property mapping

Create a reusable mapping layer.

The application must inspect the real Notion property schema and map it to website fields.

Support relevant property types such as:
- Title.
- Rich text.
- Number.
- Select.
- Status.
- Multi-select.
- Date.
- Checkbox.
- URL.
- Email.
- Phone number.
- Relation.
- Rollup.
- Formula.
- Created time.
- Last edited time.

Only write to properties supported by the API.

Treat formula and rollup fields as calculated/read-only values where appropriate.

Do not overwrite calculated properties with manually entered values.

## 7.5 Integration isolation

Each customer connection must be isolated.

Use a secure server-side customer connection record that associates:
- Customer identity.
- Notion workspace information.
- OAuth token.
- Authorized page information.
- Database IDs.
- Data-source IDs.
- Module mapping.
- Connection status.

Do not use one shared Notion access token for all customers.

Do not save customer tokens in localStorage, sessionStorage, browser cookies accessible to JavaScript, or frontend source files.

Use secure session handling and appropriate encryption for stored tokens.

